{"data":{"search":{"nodes":[{"title":"AGENTS.md and project skills removed from codex source code","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/50487","bodyText":"I just noticed that the Codex repository no longer contains AGENTS.md or the Codex self repo-specific skills, removed somewhere around 30th September. An optimistic—perhaps utopian—interpretation is that this signals a broader reassessment of how much explicit scaffolding coding agents need as models evolve. But the Astra traces I’ve seen in Langfuse still suggest a greedy approach to solving tasks: pursuing the next immediate step rather than reconsidering the overall approach. I still don’t see Astra reliably stepping back to ask what it is doing and why, even in situations where the need to reassess seems obvious.\nI have been fixated on this article https://developers.openai.com/blog/rethinking-skills-and-prompts-for-gpt-6-astra?site_locale=en and it's specific pattern of prose maneuvering, example:\nUse architecture.md for service boundaries, database.md for schema changes, and deployment.md when preparing a deployment.\n\nThis keeps bugging me, the instructions prose are slowly transitioning towards unclarity, to me, this wording leaves more implicit without clearly explaining why that is now sufficient. I haven’t seen evidence that agents can reliably interpret unstated intent but somehow this seems equivalent to male nod of communicating, nothing said but everything communicated.\nDon't get me wrong, astra is exceptional and if you use tools like vale, you can tune prose to be worded like this, but this is nothing more than finding a local minima approach or gradient descent.\nThe natural language part of NLP is just fading away.\nNoteAI was used to rephrase/jumble portions of sentences, fix grammatical issues and ambiguity.","createdAt":"2026-10-03T01:52:31Z"},{"title":"Feature request: persistent Google Drive instructions and file creation in Codex for Windows","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/48032","bodyText":"Product: Codex desktop app for Windows\nProject setup: Local Codex project with Google Drive connected through the available app/connector\nI’m working on a team project where Google Drive is the source of truth. We intentionally use an empty local project folder only as a placeholder. Project instructions prohibit reading from or writing to that local folder.\nWe need each new chat in the same Codex project to:\n\nRead the current AGENTS.md from Google Drive automatically, without requiring someone to paste its link into every chat.\nCreate and save deliverables, including CSV and JSON files, directly in authorized Google Drive folders.\nDo all of this without using the local project filesystem as a source or destination.\n\nToday, the behavior is inconsistent: some chats can read Drive and create a CSV, while other chats in the same project say they cannot find/read AGENTS.md or that creating a CSV requires a local file as its source. The connected Google account owns AGENTS.md and has editor access to the destination folders.\nSupport confirmed that Codex local does not currently document or guarantee this Drive-only workflow, and that “Sources → Add source” applies to ChatGPT Projects, not Codex local.\nCould Codex desktop support a persistent, project-level external source such as a Google Drive file/folder, plus native creation of files in Drive, with predictable availability in every new chat? If this is intentionally unsupported, please document the limitation and clarify the recommended workflow for teams that must keep project instructions and deliverables in Drive without using the local project filesystem.\nThis request concerns Codex desktop for Windows, not only Codex CLI.","createdAt":"2026-09-25T08:09:02Z"},{"title":"Agent Lint: open-source linter for Codex, AGENTS.md, MCP, Claude Code, and Cursor config","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/46874","bodyText":"I’ve open-sourced Agent Lint, a linter for configuration used by Codex and other coding agents:\nhttps://github.com/zhupanov/agent-lint\nIt validates things including:\n.codex/ and .codex-plugin/\nAGENTS.md\n.agents/skills/\nMCP configuration\nClaude Code configuration\nCursor configuration\nThe project currently has ~300 lint rules, with a documented rule registry.\nIt supports configurable rule severity and suppression, running selected rules with --only, GitHub Actions integration, and prebuilt binaries for Linux and macOS. It’s implemented in Rust.\nThe motivation was pretty simple: agent configuration is becoming large and structured enough that it increasingly benefits from the same kind of automated validation we already expect for other parts of a software project.\nI’d especially appreciate feedback from Codex users on:\nconfiguration patterns I’m not covering yet\nrules that would be useful for real-world Codex projects\nfalse positives or cases where a rule is too opinionated\nadditional AGENTS.md or MCP checks worth adding\nIssues and contributions are very welcome.","createdAt":"2026-09-20T19:27:08Z"},{"title":"ghfs: GitHub issues as read-only local files, readable from inside Codex's sandbox","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/47730","bodyText":"I build ghfs. It keeps a repository's GitHub issues on disk as Markdown files, mounted read-only at .ghfs/ in the repo, so a coding agent reads an issue with cat instead of calling GitHub in the middle of a task. Disclosure up front: it is a paid product and it is not open source. The free tier covers one repository; unlimited repositories are $5/month or $48/year.\nThe Codex-specific part is the sandbox. Codex CLI's default workspace-write sandbox runs with network access off, so gh issue view 42 mid-task cannot reach GitHub: it fails, or the agent stops to ask you to let it out of the sandbox. I checked this on macOS with Codex CLI 0.155.1, running commands through codex sandbox --permission-profile :workspace in a repo registered with ghfs:\n$ gh issue list --limit 1\nerror connecting to api.github.com\n$ cat .ghfs/github/issues/open/*.md > /dev/null && echo readable\nreadable\n\nThe files are fetched before the task starts, so reading them needs neither the network nor an approval.\nWhat goes into AGENTS.md\nghfs rules (or the prompt at the end of ghfs register) appends a section to your existing AGENTS.md, between <!-- ghfs:begin --> and <!-- ghfs:end -->. Running it again replaces only that section and leaves the rest of your file alone. The section describes the layout:\n.ghfs/\n└── {provider}/           # e.g., \"github\" or \"github-work\"\n    └── issues/\n        ├── open/\n        │   └── {number}.md   # e.g., \"42.md\"\n        └── closed/\n            └── {number}.md\n\nand tells the agent to pick one way of reading: {number}.md for one to three issues, or the hidden .index.json once for anything cross-cutting, never both in the same task.\nThe section is about 4 KB, which counts against project_doc_max_bytes (32 KiB by default). Codex also takes at most one instruction file per directory and prefers AGENTS.override.md, so if your repo root has an override, the ghfs section in AGENTS.md is not loaded and belongs in the override instead.\nLimits\nIt does not sync in real time: what is on disk is as recent as the last fetch, by command or by the background sync. Read-only is enforced by ghfs, not by the token. The device flow asks for repo scope, which GitHub treats as write-capable, and https://ghfs.dev/security#read-only covers what that does and does not mean. It is GitHub Issues only, today.\nRequirements:\n\nmacOS: Apple Silicon, macOS 26 or later, macFUSE 5.4.0 or later\nLinux: x86_64 or ARM64 with libfuse3\nWindows: inside WSL 2\n\nOn macOS, install macFUSE and enable its File System Extension in System Settings before installing ghfs. Then install with curl -fsSL https://ghfs.dev/install.sh | sh. The site is https://ghfs.dev/\nIf you point Codex at your own issues this way, I would like to hear where it gets in the way, especially with override or fallback-filename setups I have not tried.","createdAt":"2026-09-24T03:02:50Z"},{"title":"Skill Sunset: a local, read-only audit for stale AGENTS.md rules","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/41635","bodyText":"The problem\nMy AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md files kept growing, but I had no safe way to tell which instructions were still useful and which were stale, duplicated, or too broad.\nSo I built Skill Sunset, a local, read-only audit for agent instruction files. The important constraint is: TEST is not RETIRE. A suspicious rule is not removed just because a heuristic dislikes it.\nnpx skill-sunset@latest audit --codex --open\nIt scans AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, and generic SKILL.md files, then produces an evidence-backed report with conservative verdicts such as MERGE, UPDATE, DEMOTE, RETIRE, and TEST. It does not call an AI API, modify the files it scans, or delete anything.\nA reproducible example\nThe repository includes a public fixture used by the current CLI and tests. A fresh run today produced:\n\n5 files scanned\n11 findings\n71/100 health score\n0 symlinks, oversized files, or read errors skipped\n\nThe findings keep verified duplicates and broken references separate from behavior hypotheses that still need an experiment.\nThere is also one external integration signal: HOL Guard v3.0.18 added command-safety coverage for the installed skill-sunset audit surface after PR #2611 was merged.\nRepository: https://github.com/ooocooc/open-skill-sunset\nI am the project author. Parts of this write-up were drafted with AI assistance and then checked against the current CLI output and repository evidence.\nI would especially value technical counterexamples: which verdict, edge case, or instruction pattern does the tool classify incorrectly? Redacted examples are welcome.","createdAt":"2026-08-30T08:45:01Z"},{"title":"Sarge: a rule check that refuses the run, instead of another line in the instructions file","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/47107","bodyText":"Instructions files are advice. The agent reads them, agrees, and then writes the thing you told it not to — a hardcoded connection string, a duplicate route handler, await on a synchronous call.\nSarge makes the rules enforceable instead of advisory. Every file the agent writes gets judged against a rule book in the repo — by a local model, not a regex — and the run refuses while a rule is broken. The agent gets the failure back and fixes it. Break the same rule twice and a tutor writes the correction into the book, so it's already there next time.\nRuns on your own machine (Qwen3-4B, 8 GB RAM, no GPU required). Ships as a Claude Code hook and a LangGraph adapter today. MIT.\nhttps://github.com/1picassoai/Sarge\nPosting here because Agent Lint and the AGENTS.md discussions are next door to this — different approach to the same problem, and I'd be interested in what people think refusal buys over linting.","createdAt":"2026-09-21T20:41:41Z"},{"title":"Agent 007: a job board and a manager agent for Codex (and Claude Code) workers, in your browser","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/49981","bodyText":"I run a few projects with Codex and Claude Code, and the part that wore me out was not the coding. It was being the operator: opening terminals, making worktrees, checking which agent was stuck, reading their pull requests, answering the same permission prompts. So I built the operations layer and open-sourced it.\nAgent 007 (https://github.com/bill10/agent-007) is a small web app, one command (npx @bill10/agent-007), that:\n\nstarts Codex (or Claude Code, or any CLI agent) in web terminals, each in its own git worktree and branch;\nhas a job board: put a card on it and a Codex worker picks it up, works in its own worktree, and comes back with a pull request (or a summary for research cards); cards can pick a model and run on a cron schedule;\nhas one manager agent, Billion, that takes a goal, posts the cards, reads the diffs, waits for CI, merges, and asks you only about money, credentials or anything irreversible. Billion runs on Codex or Claude Code and can hand over between them when one hits its usage limit.\n\nYes, it is another orchestrator. If you already run Codex this way with scripts and AGENTS.md files, keep that; tell me what you would do differently. If you are tired of being the operator, this is the setup already built and running. Free, MIT, runs locally on your machine, works from your phone too.\nBuilt with the agents it runs: most of the code landed as pull requests from Codex and Claude Code workers on its own board, reviewed and merged by Billion.\nDemo (43 s): https://github.com/bill10/agent-007/releases/download/v0.29.0.0/billion-demo.mp4","createdAt":"2026-10-01T13:37:50Z"},{"title":"Experiment: a Codex plugin for reusable project guidance maps in AGENTS.md","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/27754","bodyText":"I built a lightweight Codex plugin experiment around repository-local guidance maps:\nhttps://github.com/Heyu2002/Code-Project-Guidance-Map\nThe plugin generates and refreshes a compact AGENTS.md action map for a codebase. It focuses on information that future Codex sessions often need before editing:\n\nmodule ownership\ntask routing\ndependency boundaries\nproject-specific editing rules\nkey entry points for each module\n\nThe goal is not to create broad project documentation or replace reading source code. The goal is to give Codex a small, durable navigation layer so it can understand large modular repositories faster and make fewer wrong assumptions.\nThis experiment was inspired by a Codex feature request I opened here:\n#26007\nFor now I’m treating this as an external/plugin-level workflow, not a request for an unsolicited core PR. I’m mainly interested in feedback from people using Codex on larger repositories:\n\nDo you run into repeated architecture rediscovery across sessions?\nWould a compact AGENTS.md module map help?\nWhat information would be most useful or dangerous to persist?\nShould this stay as a plugin-level convention, or is there value in a standardized built-in workflow later?\n\nFeedback and use cases are welcome.\nIf this resonates with your workflow, comments on either the forum thread or the GitHub issue would help clarify whether this is a broader Codex need or just a niche workflow.","createdAt":"2026-06-12T03:58:18Z"},{"title":"Make instruction loading, available capabilities, and execution evidence visible and auditable","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/47058","bodyText":"Codex users need a reliable way to determine what instructions reached the agent, what capabilities are available, and what work was actually performed. These should be standard product safeguards rather than depend on custom prompts.\nRequested improvements\n\n\nInstruction-loading report\nShow applicable instruction files, their precedence, and whether each was fully loaded, overridden, skipped, or truncated. Display the effective instruction budget and warn explicitly when guidance is omitted. The report should come from the instruction loader, rather than rely solely on the model describing its own context.\n\n\nSession capability display\nShow available tools, input access, and permission limitations. Before declaring a task impossible, assess alternative authorized methods. Distinguish unavailable, untested, failed, and permission-blocked capabilities.\n\n\nInspectable execution evidence\nFor substantive computations, provide an export connecting reported results to input identifiers, executed scripts, parameters, captured output/errors, and execution status. Distinguish calculated values from estimates and user-supplied values.\n\n\nHonest completion status\nDistinguish attempted actions, completed actions, and verified outcomes. Make tool failures, skipped requirements, placeholders, and unresolved checks visible before reporting success.\n\n\nConsistent baseline safeguards\nPreserving original data, respecting authorization, preserving acceptance criteria, and avoiding unsupported claims should be default behavior. Enforce permissions through application controls and validate these behaviors through product testing. Reporting detail and archive depth can remain configurable.\n\n\nMotivation and evidence boundaries\nThe AGENTS.md documentation describes a default 32 KiB instruction-loading budget. I have not established that my own instructions were truncated. My request is for automatic visibility so users can detect omissions without investigating internal configuration or relying on the model's assurance.\nA related experience in ChatGPT illustrates the capability-discovery problem: friends uploaded an engine video through the iPhone app and initially received a statement that its audio could not be analyzed. After a more specific prompt, ChatGPT acknowledged that it could extract and analyze the sound numerically. This is a reported ChatGPT incident, not a reproduced Codex defect, and I have not independently verified those numerical results.\nI also encountered a 5,000-character custom-instructions limit in ChatGPT web while developing reliability safeguards. This is context for the request for consistent defaults, not a claim that Codex has that same limit.\nSuggested acceptance criteria\n\nUsers can inspect loader-generated instruction status and identify omitted guidance.\nCapability refusals identify the blocked operation and alternatives checked.\nClaimed computations can be traced to captured execution evidence.\nCompletion reports disclose unfinished or unverified required work.\nFeedback can be submitted when a problem is discovered later, with a persistent link or identifier.\n\nRelevant documentation:\nhttps://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/agents-md\nIs there existing work covering these requirements, and should this proposal be split into separate tracked items?","createdAt":"2026-09-21T16:01:44Z"},{"title":"MAIOS Project Kernel: maintaining orientation as an AI agent learns and changes","url":"https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/50062","bodyText":"I'm developing MAIOS Project Kernel, an open-source semantic operating kernel for AI agents. It keeps sources, intent, competences and consequences connected so the agent can maintain orientation as its work changes: understand what the situation calls for, form useful capacities and let what happens change how it continues.\nA competence carries domain knowledge, reasons, sources and ways of working. A piece of work can produce both a result and a reusable ability. The Kernel returns that learning to the competence that should use it next, and can also change how competences are recognized, combined and formed. This is how learning can reach the method of learning itself and the system's own organization.\nOne documented editorial event illustrates this at a bounded scale. An observation about returning to a topic from a changed present altered both the article's argument and the writing method represented for later work. The source trace follows the observation, participating competences, artifact and acquired method; later use remains a separate observation.\nThe public repository contains the Kernel's operating knowledge, competence methods, a deterministic package builder and an installer. The installer provides a project-local Codex profile using .agents/skills; the compatibility guide describes the host paths and discovery conditions. For source study, the study competence can be read directly without installation.\nYou can begin by studying the source with your own agent:\n\n\nClone the repository and open its root with Codex.\n\n\nAsk it:\n\n\nRead AGENTS.md and skills/maios-kernel-study/SKILL.md.\nUse that competence to help me understand the Kernel.\nFollow one concrete example from the current sources: how does work change\nthe knowledge or competence used next?\n\nIf you want to use it in your own context, the installation route starts from the selected target folder and preserves existing work.\nThis public community is at its beginning. I'd welcome questions about the operating relation, observations from source study, and contributions that make a method or explanation more useful. The contribution guide explains how to contribute.\nMAIOS grows from the D-ND programme. The System Semantic Kernel working-paper corpus develops the relation among meaning, competence, operation and consequence, including how a system can transform its own generative methods across different receivers and media.\nThe D-ND manifesto sets out the wider trajectory: intelligence that maintains its orientation as it becomes more capable. Today's cognitive kernels provide a working field for research into models formed natively through these relations. A natively formed LLM-D-ND remains a research direction, with its implementation open.","createdAt":"2026-10-01T18:16:31Z"}]}}}